1
00:00:00,320 --> 00:00:06,350
Okay, so now we need to change the direction of our car so it doesn't just look like it's gliding across

2
00:00:06,350 --> 00:00:07,910
the screen but actually turning.

3
00:00:08,360 --> 00:00:14,180
And to achieve this, like with our other project we can use this point direction block.

4
00:00:14,770 --> 00:00:17,620
But you might have noticed something strange if you try to use it.

5
00:00:17,740 --> 00:00:23,260
And that is if you point in a straight direction and click it, you'll see our car point to the side,

6
00:00:23,260 --> 00:00:24,970
which seems rather weird.

7
00:00:25,240 --> 00:00:27,220
But there is some sense to this.

8
00:00:27,610 --> 00:00:32,770
Scratch assumes that your sprites default direction is facing to the right.

9
00:00:33,620 --> 00:00:36,050
Let's take the default cat sprite as an example.

10
00:00:36,080 --> 00:00:40,850
When you bring it into your project, you'll see that it really looks like it's facing right.

11
00:00:41,420 --> 00:00:50,360
So when you use a block to make it face up or zero degrees, it's actually looks like it's facing up

12
00:00:50,360 --> 00:00:53,510
because it was initially facing the right.

13
00:00:53,540 --> 00:00:59,570
Now, I know it's still a bit confusing, but the key here is to understand that scratch assumes all

14
00:00:59,570 --> 00:01:01,580
your sprites are facing this way.

15
00:01:01,670 --> 00:01:08,480
So when you create your own sprite or use a different one, just make sure to adjust its initial direction

16
00:01:08,480 --> 00:01:13,220
in the costume editor so it faces the right way when you start working with it.

17
00:01:13,730 --> 00:01:21,140
So moving back to our car, we can go into the editor and using this arrow, we can rotate our car to

18
00:01:21,140 --> 00:01:22,340
have it face right.

19
00:01:23,010 --> 00:01:24,240
And here you have it.

20
00:01:24,270 --> 00:01:27,990
Now you can see that it points in the exact direction that we set it.

21
00:01:28,020 --> 00:01:28,830
Great.

22
00:01:29,500 --> 00:01:32,800
Now let's implement this into our code.

23
00:01:32,800 --> 00:01:39,910
So quite simply what we want is to look like it's turning right when we click the right arrow and left

24
00:01:39,910 --> 00:01:42,250
when we click the left arrow.

25
00:01:42,900 --> 00:01:48,570
So we can just drag our point direction and put one in each of these if statements.

26
00:01:49,310 --> 00:01:53,360
So for the first we can have it at about 15 degrees.

27
00:01:54,050 --> 00:01:57,230
And for the second, we can set it to -50.

28
00:01:57,770 --> 00:02:02,810
Now, if we hit play again and then turn, you can see it works.

29
00:02:02,930 --> 00:02:10,310
Now, although there are clearly a few bugs here, the first is quite easy to dress, which is when

30
00:02:10,310 --> 00:02:11,060
we start the game.

31
00:02:11,060 --> 00:02:15,770
Our car kind of points to the side, which doesn't quite look right.

32
00:02:16,040 --> 00:02:21,500
So we can just add a point direction to the start and have it point straight on the start.

33
00:02:21,800 --> 00:02:24,020
And now, as you can see, that's much better.

34
00:02:24,020 --> 00:02:29,390
Although as you can also see, if we stop turning and just go straight, our car still points in whatever

35
00:02:29,390 --> 00:02:31,130
direction it was last pointing.

36
00:02:31,730 --> 00:02:37,820
Now this is a bit more complex, as we need a way for a car to point straight if none of the buttons

37
00:02:37,820 --> 00:02:38,450
are clicked.

38
00:02:38,950 --> 00:02:46,570
So once again, pause the video and try to see if you can get this to work and get the car to point

39
00:02:46,570 --> 00:02:47,260
straight.

40
00:02:48,380 --> 00:02:49,460
Did you figure it out?

41
00:02:51,020 --> 00:02:56,930
Now looking at our code, you can see we have these two if statements that check if we are clicking

42
00:02:56,930 --> 00:02:58,250
one key or the other key.

43
00:02:58,520 --> 00:03:02,090
But there is another type of if statement that we haven't really used.

44
00:03:02,090 --> 00:03:04,280
And it's so super important.

45
00:03:04,800 --> 00:03:09,690
So if we're going down to our control, you will see we have this if else block.

46
00:03:10,170 --> 00:03:17,010
And well we know what the if block does like if a condition is true it will execute some code.

47
00:03:17,010 --> 00:03:19,770
Like if the right arrow is clicked, it will turn right.

48
00:03:19,770 --> 00:03:21,990
And with this block it will do the same.

49
00:03:21,990 --> 00:03:28,170
But if the condition is not true, it will execute any code in this else block.

50
00:03:28,760 --> 00:03:30,560
So let's see this in action.

51
00:03:30,860 --> 00:03:34,940
Firstly, taking all this code out we can swap it for this.

52
00:03:35,240 --> 00:03:42,920
And here we can bring in if the right arrow is clicked and then point the direction and change x.

53
00:03:43,880 --> 00:03:45,350
In for the elves.

54
00:03:45,380 --> 00:03:47,330
Let's just bring in this other code.

55
00:03:47,870 --> 00:03:53,600
Now, if you hit play, you will see that if you click the right arrow, the car does what is expected.

56
00:03:53,930 --> 00:03:59,630
But if the right arrow is not clicked, the car glides to the left.

57
00:04:00,560 --> 00:04:03,050
So now we understand how all that works.

58
00:04:03,050 --> 00:04:06,620
Let's bring it back to how it was and a useful tip.

59
00:04:07,100 --> 00:04:09,470
Just click command Z or control Z.

60
00:04:09,470 --> 00:04:12,470
If you're on windows and you'll see your code revert to how it was.

61
00:04:12,470 --> 00:04:13,580
So let's do this.

62
00:04:14,550 --> 00:04:18,330
Let's swap out this left arrow for this if else.

63
00:04:19,030 --> 00:04:21,610
And then move the code inside.

64
00:04:22,330 --> 00:04:29,110
And finally in the else we can bring in a point direction and have it point straight.

65
00:04:29,880 --> 00:04:31,860
Well, that makes sense.

66
00:04:32,130 --> 00:04:34,470
If we click right, it points right.

67
00:04:34,620 --> 00:04:36,540
If we click left, it points left.

68
00:04:36,570 --> 00:04:38,820
Otherwise it points up.

69
00:04:38,940 --> 00:04:40,290
So let's give that a try.

70
00:04:41,510 --> 00:04:44,690
Well, that's not right, is it?

71
00:04:45,350 --> 00:04:47,090
Let's try to untangle this a bit.

72
00:04:47,120 --> 00:04:52,340
We have a situation where clicking right doesn't seem to work as expected, but clicking left does.

73
00:04:53,280 --> 00:04:57,810
The answer lies in the order in which the code is processed.

74
00:04:57,840 --> 00:05:02,730
Remember, in programming, code is run from the top to the bottom.

75
00:05:02,760 --> 00:05:06,000
It checks each condition in the order they appear.

76
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:10,530
So when we click write, our sprite is indeed instructed to point right.

77
00:05:10,620 --> 00:05:16,650
However, there is more code after this that the computer still has to check, which is this here.

78
00:05:17,010 --> 00:05:19,860
If we click left, it will point left.

79
00:05:19,860 --> 00:05:27,510
But you see here, if this condition is not true, whatever is in this block will happen.

80
00:05:27,510 --> 00:05:31,410
The final block of code gives a command for the sprite to point straight.

81
00:05:31,950 --> 00:05:37,500
The sprite will follow this command if this condition is not met, and because this block is last,

82
00:05:37,500 --> 00:05:40,530
it's ultimately what will be executed.

83
00:05:41,280 --> 00:05:46,890
Yeah, I know programming logic is quite something, but the more you really try to understand it,

84
00:05:46,890 --> 00:05:48,360
the more clear it all becomes.

85
00:05:48,360 --> 00:05:51,330
It's all about thinking like a computer.

86
00:05:52,060 --> 00:05:54,010
So that all said, how do we fix this?

87
00:05:54,520 --> 00:06:00,610
Well, in programming, you generally don't want to have a situation where there are if blocks in a

88
00:06:00,610 --> 00:06:01,930
single script or loop.

89
00:06:01,930 --> 00:06:06,130
Instead, what we want to use is an if else.

90
00:06:06,280 --> 00:06:14,470
So instead of this code looking like this, we will grab another if else, put this code inside.

91
00:06:15,610 --> 00:06:16,900
Put it in the loop.

92
00:06:17,530 --> 00:06:22,630
And finally we can put this if else in this else statement.

93
00:06:23,230 --> 00:06:25,510
So not if we look at the code.

94
00:06:25,510 --> 00:06:28,990
If the right arrow is clicked, this will execute.

95
00:06:29,170 --> 00:06:33,850
Otherwise, if the left arrow is clicked, this code will execute.

96
00:06:33,850 --> 00:06:38,380
And then only if none of the above conditions are true.

97
00:06:38,470 --> 00:06:40,600
No left or right is clicked.

98
00:06:40,750 --> 00:06:42,730
Our car will point straight.

99
00:06:43,090 --> 00:06:44,920
So playing the game again.

100
00:06:44,920 --> 00:06:46,720
See now how it runs perfectly.

101
00:06:47,810 --> 00:06:55,730
So adding in this if else ensures that this bottom code can only execute if we aren't clicking the right

102
00:06:55,730 --> 00:06:59,810
arrow, and then only if we aren't also clicking the left arrow.

103
00:06:59,840 --> 00:07:03,620
Then finally will this else execute.

104
00:07:04,410 --> 00:07:09,480
Now, all this might be a little bit confusing, but this is really the essence of programming.

105
00:07:09,480 --> 00:07:16,320
Just understanding the order of operations and how code is executed and organized logically.

106
00:07:16,320 --> 00:07:20,580
And trust me, the more you build, the clearer it will become.

107
00:07:20,580 --> 00:07:26,190
So play around with it and try to understand it because they are exciting things to come.

